## Key Design Decisions

### Dependency Injection
Every class accepts pre-built clients (`Spot`, `UMFutures`, `redis.StrictRedis`, `MongoClient`) via its constructor. This makes unit testing straightforward — pass mocks in, no monkey-patching required.

### Redis as Shared State
Trade state, order-to-trade mappings, cooldowns, and sentiment scores all live in Redis. Because all workers run in separate threads within the same process, Redis provides a consistent, thread-safe store without requiring Python-level locks.

### Algo/Conditional Orders for SL/TP
Stop-loss and take-profit orders are placed via Binance's `/fapi/v1/algoOrder` endpoint (`CONDITIONAL` type) rather than standard orders. This allows server-side trigger logic and avoids the `-4045` duplicate-stop-order error. The `ensure_orders` self-healing mechanism re-creates any missing SL/TP orders on every 5-minute reconciliation cycle.

### Lazy Strategy Registry
`_LazyStrategyRegistry` defers importing strategy classes until first access, keeping startup time fast regardless of how many strategies are registered.

### Watchdog Without Auto-Restart
`monitor_workers()` checks thread liveness every 300 seconds and sends a Discord alert if any worker has stopped. It does **not** auto-restart threads — this is intentional to avoid masking persistent errors. Manual intervention or a process supervisor (e.g. `systemd`, `supervisord`) is expected for restarts.

### Locale-Based Endpoint Selection
`AuthenticationManager` and `MongoHandler` both check `locale.getdefaultlocale()` to select `api.binance.us` (US locale) vs `api.binance.com` (all others).

